  "escuchar.apartado": "Listen to this section", "pdf.generando": "Preparing the PDF…",
  "ayuda.datos.t": "Basic details", "ayuda.datos.x": "<p>With your age, falls and clinical situation the app adjusts its recommendations: training at 40 is not the same as at 80, nor with diabetes as without it.</p><p>Fill in only what you know. Anything left blank is ignored and blocks nothing.</p>",
  "ayuda.edad.t": "Why do we ask your age?", "ayuda.edad.x": "<p>From 65 onwards fall prevention and strength work become priorities, and some drugs need more care (Beers and STOPP/START criteria).</p><p>It is also used to estimate your maximum heart rate and intensity zones.</p><p><b>Example:</b> 72.</p>",
  "ayuda.fcreposo.t": "Resting heart rate", "ayuda.fcreposo.x": "<p>Count your pulse for one minute in the morning, seated and calm, or check your watch or band.</p><p>With it the app works out your light, moderate and vigorous heart-rate zones (Karvonen method). If you don't know it, an average value is used.</p><p><b>Example:</b> 64 bpm.</p>",
  "ayuda.caidas.t": "Falls in the last year", "ayuda.caidas.x": "<p>Any fall to the ground counts, even without injury. Having fallen once multiplies the risk of falling again, and some drugs (sedatives, blood-pressure lowering) increase it further.</p><p>If ticked, the plan prioritises supported balance work and leg strength.</p>",
  "ayuda.sppb.t": "Physical performance test (SPPB)", "ayuda.sppb.x": "<p>Three simple 5-minute tests: standing balance, a 4-metre walk and standing up from a chair 5 times. Scored 0 to 12.</p><p>The app guides you step by step with a timer. Do it with someone nearby. The result adjusts your plan: with 9 points or fewer, strength and balance come first.</p>",
  "ayuda.situacion.t": "Clinical situation", "ayuda.situacion.x": "<p>Tick only what you have been diagnosed with. Each situation activates its own recommendations: with diabetes, watch your glucose; with an aneurysm, avoid maximal loads; with atrial fibrillation, don't rely on your pulse…</p><p>If none apply, leave it blank.</p>",
  "ayuda.cribado.t": "Safety screening", "ayuda.cribado.x": "<p>Three questions from the American College of Sports Medicine (ACSM 2015) to tell whether a medical check is advisable before starting or increasing intensity.</p><p>It is not an exam and blocks nothing: it only guides. If you have symptoms such as chest pain or breathlessness on small efforts, see a doctor before training.</p>",
  "ayuda.niveles.t": "What the levels mean", "ayuda.niveles.x": "<ul class='niveles-ayuda'><li class='nivel-alerta'><b>Caution</b>: something important to consider before training or to discuss with your doctor; it does not mean you cannot exercise.</li><li class='nivel-precaucion'><b>Precaution</b>: a specific adaptation (how, when or how much) to train safely.</li><li class='nivel-info'><b>Good to know</b>: useful information that does not change the plan.</li></ul><p>Interactions are classed as major, moderate or minor according to the risk of the combination.</p>",
  "tour.paso": "Step {n} of {t}", "tour.sig": "Next", "tour.fin": "Get started",
  "tour.datos.t": "First, your details", "tour.datos.x": "Age, falls and clinical situation. Fill in only what you know; the «?» next to each section explains it.",
